.Dd September 23, 2026
.Dt RELIC 1
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm relic
.Nd evaluate Datalog programs and answer queries
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl aceinsv
.Op Fl F Ar format
.Op Fl l Ar limit Ns = Ns Ar value
.Op Fl o Ar file
.Op Fl q Ar query
.Op Fl X Ar debug
.Op Ar
.Nm
.Fl V
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The
.Nm
utility loads a Datalog program, checks it, and answers its queries.
The program is read from each
.Ar file
in the order given, all of them forming one program, or from the standard
input when no
.Ar file
is given and the standard input is not a terminal.
.Pp
A program is a sequence of facts, rules and queries.
A fact is an atom ending in a period; a rule is an atom, the
.Ql :-
token and a body of literals separated by commas, ending in a period; a
query is a body ending in a question mark:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
edge(a, b).
edge(b, c).
path(X, Y) :- edge(X, Y).
path(X, Z) :- edge(X, Y), path(Y, Z).
path(a, X)?
.Ed
.Pp
A symbol begins with a lowercase letter, a variable with an uppercase
letter or an underscore, and
.Ql _
alone matches anything.
Integers are decimal and may be negative.
Strings are written in double quotes and may hold any bytes, spelled with
the escapes
.Ql \e\e ,
.Ql \e\(dq ,
.Ql \en ,
.Ql \et ,
.Ql \er ,
.Ql \e0
and
.Ql \exHH .
A body literal is an atom, an atom preceded by
.Ql not ,
or a comparison with one of
.Ql = ,
.Ql != ,
.Ql < ,
.Ql <= ,
.Ql >
and
.Ql >= .
A comparison that orders values of different types is false.
.Ql %
begins a comment that runs to the end of the line.
.Pp
Every variable of a rule's head, of a negated literal and of a comparison
must also occur in a positive atom of the body, and a program may not be
recursive through negation.
A program that breaks either rule is rejected and nothing is evaluated.
.Pp
The options are as follows:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl a
Evaluate the whole program before answering any query, rather than answering
each query through a rewrite that derives only what its constants can
reach.
The answers are the same.
.It Fl c
Check the program and its queries, and evaluate nothing.
.It Fl e
Write the plan of everything evaluated to the standard error: each part of
the program as it is planned, and each query's plan before its rows.
A part whose predicates have facts counts them as
.Ql facts: N ;
a predicate of facts alone needs no evaluation and is not shown.
The text is meant to be read and may change from one release to the next.
A plan names a query's answer
.Ql $q
and a serial counting every query prepared, from zero, which need not be the
number diagnostics and
.Fl v
give it.
.It Fl F Ar format
Write query results in
.Ar format :
.Cm text ,
the default,
.Cm csv ,
.Cm tsv
or
.Cm json .
The formats are described in
.Sx OUTPUT .
.It Fl i
Once the queries have run, read queries from the standard input and answer
each in turn, as described in
.Sx INTERACTIVE MODE .
The standard input is then never read as a program, and with no
.Ar file
the program is empty.
A run that fails before the session would begin ends without one.
.It Fl l Ar limit Ns = Ns Ar value
Set a resource limit.
The option may be repeated, and a limit given twice takes the later value.
The limits are described in
.Sx RESOURCE LIMITS .
.It Fl n
Run every query and write none of its rows.
The output is never opened, so
.Fl o
creates no file.
With
.Fl v
this counts each query's answers; with
.Fl e
it shows what each query runs.
.It Fl o Ar file
Write query results to
.Ar file ,
created or truncated, instead of the standard output.
The file is opened only once the program and every query have been checked,
so a run that fails its checks leaves an existing file alone.
.It Fl q Ar query
Answer
.Ar query ,
written as in a program and ending in a question mark, instead of the
queries the program contains.
The option may be repeated, and the queries are answered in the order
given; diagnostics about the third are named
.Ql <query 3> .
The program's own queries are still checked.
.It Fl s
Sort each query's rows before writing them, column by column: integers
before symbols before strings, integers numerically, and symbols and
strings bytewise.
Without
.Fl s
the order of the rows is unspecified, though the same on every run over the
same input.
.It Fl V
Print the version and exit.
.It Fl v
Say more on the standard error.
Once: after each query that succeeds, a line
.Ql query N: M rows ,
where N is the query's number: its position among the
.Fl q
queries, or else the program's own, continued in an interactive session as
.Sx INTERACTIVE MODE
describes.
Twice: also a note for the first comparison in each rule that ordered values
of different types, which is false and easily unintended.
.It Fl X Ar debug
Select an internal behaviour, for testing and debugging.
The option may be repeated.
These are not part of the stable interface:
.Bl -tag -width no-reorder
.It Cm naive
evaluate recursion by the naive fixed point;
.It Cm no-index
build no index, scanning instead;
.It Cm no-magic
answer every query from the whole program;
.It Cm no-reorder
join a rule's atoms in the order they are written;
.It Cm stats
write the evaluation counters to the standard error when the run ends;
the derivations count what rules derive and the facts copied into a
predicate that rules also derive, not the facts of a predicate no rule
derives.
.El
.Pp
Each gives the same answers as the default.
.El
.Pp
An invocation checks everything before it evaluates anything: every file is
loaded and every query prepared, and all the mistakes found are reported,
before the first query runs.
The queries then run in turn, and the first that fails ends the run.
.Sh RESOURCE LIMITS
Each
.Fl l
limit is a decimal
.Ar value
from 0, which asks for the default, to the largest size the platform can
hold.
.Bl -tag -width magic-preds
.It Cm tuples
the most tuples all relations together may hold, the program's facts
included; by default there is no limit.
.It Cm memory
the most bytes relation and index storage, the program's facts included,
the plans of an evaluation and the rewrite of a query may occupy together;
by default there is no limit.
A long rule's plans can need more memory than the data does.
.It Cm magic-preds
the most predicates the rewrite of one query may generate.
.It Cm magic-rules
the most rules the rewrite of one query may generate.
.El
.Pp
A query or evaluation that exceeds
.Cm tuples
or
.Cm memory
fails, and writes no rows.
A file whose facts would exceed either is refused when it is loaded, with
nothing of it added, and the diagnostic names the first predicate, in the
order the file names them, that would gain facts:
.Dl relic: error: limit exceeded: tuples=4 while loading e/1
When neither
.Cm magic
limit is set, the rewrite has budgets in proportion to the program, on the
predicates it generates and on the literals of the rules it drafts, which
bound the rules it keeps as well, and a rewrite that outgrows one is
abandoned with a warning and the query answered from the whole program.
Setting either lifts every budget: a
.Cm magic
limit that is set is a limit like the others, and one left unset beside it
bounds nothing.
.Sh INTERACTIVE MODE
With
.Fl i ,
each line of the standard input is one of:
.Bl -bullet
.It
nothing but blanks, or a comment beginning with
.Ql % ,
which is ignored;
.It
a command beginning with a period;
.It
one query, which must fit on the line.
.El
.Pp
A query is answered as
.Fl q
answers one, into the same output and format.
Only queries are accepted; facts and rules are not.
The commands are:
.Bl -tag -width ".plan query"
.It Ic .help
List the commands.
.It Ic .plan Ar query
Run
.Ar query
and write its plan instead of its rows, to the standard output even under
.Fl e .
.It Ic .quit
End the session.
.It Ic .stats
Write the evaluation counters.
.El
.Pp
A command's reply is written to the standard output, never to the
.Fl o
file.
Each query of the session, and each
.Ic .plan ,
is numbered after the queries that ran before the session, and a query
that is rejected still takes its number.
A query or command that fails is reported, and the session goes on.
The session ends at the end of input, at
.Ic .quit ,
or when a read or a write fails.
.Pp
When the standard input and the standard error are both a terminal, the
session prompts with
.Ql relic>
on the standard error and, where
.Nm
was built with libedit, allows each line to be edited and recalled.
Otherwise there is no prompt, so that a session read from a file or a pipe
writes its answers and nothing else.
Everything answered is written out before the session waits for the next
line.
.Sh OUTPUT
Every format writes one row per answer.
A row's columns are the query's variables, in the order they first occur.
A query with no variables writes one empty row when it holds and nothing
when it does not.
In
.Cm text ,
.Cm csv
and
.Cm tsv ,
the rows of each query but the first are preceded by an empty line.
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Cm text
Values separated by one space, each written as a program would write it:
integers in decimal, symbols as they are, and strings in double quotes with
the escapes of the language.
A string's control characters, C1 controls included, and any byte that is
not valid UTF-8 are escaped, so results are always safe to print.
.It Cm csv
RFC 4180 fields separated by commas, each record ending in a newline.
A field is quoted when it holds a comma, a double quote, a carriage return
or a newline, or is empty.
Symbols and strings are written as their bytes, unescaped.
.It Cm tsv
Fields separated by one tab.
A backslash, tab, newline and carriage return inside a value are written as
.Ql \e\e ,
.Ql \et ,
.Ql \en
and
.Ql \er .
.It Cm json
One document per query: an array of rows, each an array of values, one row
per line.
An integer is a number and a symbol a string holding its bytes; a string
value is a JSON string holding the
.Cm text
format's quoted literal, so a JSON string beginning with a double quote is
a string value and any other is a symbol.
.El
.Pp
Diagnostics, plans, counts and counters are written to the standard error,
so the standard output holds only results and, in an interactive session,
the replies to its commands.
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width ~/.editrc
.It Pa ~/.editrc
libedit's configuration, read when an interactive session edits its lines;
see
.Xr editrc 5 .
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Ex -std
It exits 1 when the program or a query was rejected, when evaluation failed,
when anything in an interactive session failed, or when input or output
failed, and 2 when the command line is malformed.
.Sh EXAMPLES
Check a program without evaluating it:
.Pp
.Dl $ relic -c project.dl
.Pp
Answer the queries written in two files that form one program:
.Pp
.Dl $ relic rules.dl facts.dl
.Pp
Answer a query given on the command line, as CSV:
.Pp
.Dl $ relic -F csv -q 'depends(X, Y)?' project.dl
.Pp
Show how a query is answered, without its rows:
.Pp
.Dl $ relic -n -e -q 'path(a, X)?' graph.dl
.Pp
Bound the work a query may do:
.Pp
.Dl $ relic -l tuples=5000000 -l memory=2147483648 -q 'path(X, Y)?' graph.dl
.Pp
Explore a program interactively:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ relic -i graph.dl
relic> path(a, X)?
b
c
relic> .quit
.Ed
.Sh DIAGNOSTICS
A diagnostic about the program names the file, line and column it concerns,
as
.Ql graph.dl:4:22: error: message .
Any byte that is not printable ASCII in a diagnostic is escaped.
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr relic 3 ,
.Xr editrc 5
.Sh CAVEATS
.Cm csv
and
.Cm tsv
write a string's bytes as they are, control characters included.
They are meant for programs to read; the results of an untrusted program in
either format, printed to a terminal, can carry escape sequences.
